WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.800
Which loses more of the cortex: an eye that sends nothing, or an eye that sends

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.800
noise?

3
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.150
Lid suture blurs an eye's image.

4
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.910
But its retina keeps firing under the lid.

5
00:00:14.883 --> 00:00:20.943
In 1986, Michael Stryker's laboratory asked whether electrical activity itself is

6
00:00:20.943 --> 00:00:21.943
what counts.

7
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:27.373
They silenced one eye of kittens with tetrodotoxin, a toxin that blocks sodium

8
00:00:27.373 --> 00:00:28.373
channels.

9
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:32.987
It silences the retinal ganglion cells, the eye's output neurons.

10
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.490
Cortical responses shifted toward the eye that kept firing.

11
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.510
So an imbalance of electrical activity between the eyes is enough to cause a shift.

12
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.910
In the same year, Stryker and William Harris silenced both eyes during development.

13
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:53.373
Then the two eyes' axons failed to separate into stripes.

14
00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:57.157
Now there are two ways to take an eye out of the contest.

15
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.710
A sutured eye, whose retina still fires.

16
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.910
And a silenced eye, which sends nothing at all.

17
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:10.053
Predict. If the more active eye simply wins, which closed eye should lose more?

18
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:11.953
What does our rule predict?

19
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:21.823
By amount, the silenced eye is quieter, so it should lose more.

20
00:01:22.743 --> 00:01:23.743
It loses less.

21
00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:29.923
In kittens, lid suture weakens the closed eye's responses more than tetrodotoxin

22
00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:30.923
does.

23
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:39.067
Why? The sutured eye's retina is still active under the lid.

24
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:42.717
That leftover activity is unpatterned.

25
00:01:43.277 --> 00:01:45.117
It is out of step with the cells it drives.

26
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.607
And it seems to weaken the eye's own synapses.

27
00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:52.637
A silent eye is never active at the wrong moments.

28
00:01:53.177 --> 00:01:54.377
So it loses less.

29
00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:58.737
So the rule that the more active eye wins fails this test.

30
00:01:59.537 --> 00:02:00.657
So does simple disuse.

31
00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.127
The rule in the box can account for the difference.

32
00:02:05.007 --> 00:02:10.007
The sutured eye's input is the kind its weakening half acts on; a silent input is

33
00:02:10.007 --> 00:02:11.107
left nearly alone.

34
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.790
The open eye drives the cell's strong responses.

35
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.430
On its own, the sutured eye evokes only weak ones.

36
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.960
Takeaway: silence is not the same as noise.

37
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.300
An eye whose activity is out of step with the cell loses more than an eye that is

38
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.300
simply silent.

39
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:30.393
One question remains.

40
00:02:31.353 --> 00:02:33.053
Why does all of this work mainly in the young?
